00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  if you're an adult in dc or united  states you probably have a credit score  and you've probably been told maybe more  than once to live your life in such a  way that will improve that strange  number that lies somewhere between 300  and 850. build good credit if you're  told and financial success will follow  the higher your credit score the happier  and more secure you will be  that is not a wholly irrational belief  credit scores have a pervasive though  often invisible set of impacts on our  lives they are everywhere today they're  often required for renting an apartment  for getting a mortgage for taking out a  loan even for getting a job but for a  number so important it can be remarkably  arbitrary you've probably experienced  just how capricious and downright weird  your credit score can be pay off a loan  do the responsible thing and your credit  score may go  down not up but maintain multiple credit  cards use them frequently and  continually pay installment loans your  credit score is likely to  increase as long as you never miss a  payment  it is a very strange system now believe  it or not it wasn't always like this  this peculiar practice where a score is  so powerful in determining so many  aspects of your life wasn't always  around in fact credit scores also known  as fico scores after the company that  created them weren't used to determine  loan pricing until  1989.  that's not that long ago  how did it get this way how did we come  to a point where this funny little  number exerts so much power over our  daily lives  to understand we'll have to go back a  bit to a time called the 1970s it was  the age of bell-bottom jeans and disco  gerald ford god bless him and dog day  afternoon dog day afternoon not just al  pacino but john cazale and charles  dirting  but the 1970s was also a time when  american industry was getting rapidly  automated and outsourced and a lot of  people who had previously made a pretty  good living began to lose out throughout  the 70s and going into the 80s 90s and  the aughts industries like steel  production or auto manufacturing rapidly  shed their workers and places like  youngstown or flint or warren ohio  that's my dad's hometown we're left  without a whole lot of support  manufacturing fell from more than 30  percent of total employment in 1955 down  to 22  1975 and then less than 19 percent by  1985. by 2005 it had fallen to just  barely above  10  of the total jobs in this country for  the most part these high-wage industrial  jobs with good pensions and benefits  were replaced by jobs in the growing  service sector  a lot of people in our great national  industrial belt wound up working at  places like walmart or mcdonald's which  provided no benefits or pension and  wages a lot lower than what people were  previously making at the same time as  this process of de-industrialization was  unfolding a new philosophy was on the  rise in government  it was an attitude that prioritized belt  tightening  austerity deregulation an approach that  was called  neo-liberalism  as many unemployed or underemployed  workers grew into a surplus population  on the margins of the economy political  elites slashed welfare programs while  deregulating the industries like banking  and in 1978 the supreme court prevented  states from enforcing laws capping  interest rates for nationally chartered  banks gutting anti-usury protections for  lenders so  the 1970s saw two important trends first  de-industrialization and falling living  standards in much of the united states  and second the deregulation of banking  and lending in the 1980s these two  trends would collide you see with the  new glut of households that were  dependent on low wage no benefit jobs  people could no longer survive just on  their incomes as they might have been  able to before so they had to turn to  the exciting new world of consumer  credit and debt became a bigger and  bigger part of working-class life  this led to what the scholar suzanne  soderbergh calls  debt fair  consumer debt as a substitute for good  jobs and good wages and a social safety  net now of course debt has been around  for a very long time and was already  widely used in the united states to  finance big ticket purchases like cars  or college  but as wages collapsed and welfare  programs were cut  more and more families were forced to go  deeply into debt just to stay afloat  the cold hard reality was that people  just couldn't live on what they earned  and were constantly on a treadmill of  debt in order to provide for their  families  in the 1980s banks learned how to take  advantage of this situation before the  credit cards they offered were a loss  leader for them  but then they realized something  important the way to make money was not  to target people who were well off and  could pay off their debt no it was to  target people who are not well off who  were reliant on debt and who could not  pay it all off at once as one banker  said in an interview and i quote  it didn't require a lot of investigation  to see that the people who paid their  debt in full every month were not  profitable banks called these customers  revolvers  people who took on the maximal amount of  debt they could without defaulting and  who could be charged higher interest  rates as a result  overwhelmingly of course these revolvers  were living paycheck to paycheck  who needed debt in order to get by in  the harsh new economy they became the  target really the prey for an explosion  in consumer lending with the plastic  credit card at its center the banks  pitched it as a matter of freedom and  flexibility but for the downwardly  mobile people who were targeted it was  about necessity  the massive expansion of consumer  lending is where the credit score came  in see lenders had long employed ways to  discern how creditworthy their  applicants were with arbitrary judgments  of whether they were  shifty or  of good character  but as corporations amassed huge reams  of information on customers and their  behavior they now had enough  computerized information for them to  cook up algorithms about who was and who  was not credit worthy so in 1975 the  first fico score was developed  but the banks weren't so interested in  trustworthy or creditworthy lenders no  they wanted those revolvers the people  who would make them as much money as  possible and when the first general  purpose fico score came out in 1989  that's what it was designed around which  is why in 1995 the director of consumer  policy at experian one of the biggest  credit rating agencies admitted  modeling just credit risk is passe the  real risk is that a customer is not  profitable  they were searching for what one banking  publication called  debt prone consumers and systematically  targeting working class people who now  needed debt in order to survive and who  could be charged higher and higher  interest rates as a result so starting  in the 1980s lending to the poor  so-called subprime or non-prime lending  grew rapidly  the riskier the borrowers were the more  interest they could be charged and the  more money the bank could make off them  that's why at wells fargo one of the  biggest banks in america loan officers  were incentivized for years to  aggressively market subprime loans to  black communities even for people who  qualified for prime loans at lower  interest rates these victims were the  profitable customers the banks were  looking for wells fargo called these  ghetto loans and loan officers referred  to the individuals they were trapping in  debt as  mud people  the goal of course was never to help  them it was to make  money off them through predatory lending  the credit score became a crucial part  of this it's a tool of discipline that  makes you into the ideal customer for  the banks that's why credit scores don't  reflect actual financial health things  like the difference between your income  and your spending or how much money  you've saved what they do reflect is you  being a reliable  juicy profit source for them they're not  looking for trustworthy borrowers  they're looking for people who can be  counted on to live in debt because  keeping you in debt means profit your  credit score rewards you not for making  decisions that actually help you  financially but for taking on the  maximum amount of debt that you can  possibly repay  in an economy as unequal and predatory  as ours credit scores may be necessary  in the narrow sense of what our system  demands  but credit scores and the entire system  of debt that lies underneath them are  not a fact of nature they're not  something eternal like the thespian  artistry of al pacino credit scores are  necessary only in an economy that  cannibalizes the hard work sweat and  tears of its working people that  regulate so much of our lives through a  number reflecting your utility to the  banking industry you can spend your  whole life working on that number and  many of you will  but no matter how high you get that  score no matter how many years of work  and sacrifice you spend  you will have already paid the price  i'm chase madar wandering law professor  for the grivel institute  [Music]  [Music]  you

